# Complete parity acceptance

Status: active. Continue implementation and verification until the supported
Decko contract is met. A reviewed repair batch or a partial score does not
complete the port. Optimization follows established parity and must preserve it.

For current execution, [the shared-data comparison decision](2026-10-31-shared-data-engine-comparison.md)
requires our existing Ruby copy and TypeScript/Bunny to use the same controlled
data. A newer private live-site export and historical serving ancestry are not
prerequisites for general engine completion. The retained consumer walk below
remains history and downstream cutover evidence; it must not create a circular
dependency between the engine and its consumer.

## Immediate evidence

The consuming project's retained walk has 57 paired observations, identified by
request, path, capture and backend in its private evidence file. Its input
SHA-256 is `5078c06ac881b9cf0052b12a13785910e828df4886911680f6babcfee571a937`.
These observations are distinct from the synthetic 51-journey instrument.
The current source, installed copy, database snapshot and checker identities
must be recorded separately. An older deployed bundle is not evidence about
the repaired source, and a newer source test is not deployed acceptance.

All retained mismatches remain in the report. Attribute each to an engine
behavior, serving configuration, missing or changed data, an intentional
consumer change, or an unresolved cause. Under the operator's site-comparison
decisions of 2026-10-29 and 2026-10-30, content-only differences from the
September snapshot count as cutover matches without another proof round.
Retain the raw difference and its content classification. Rendering and
behavior differences remain repair work, including wrong navigation targets,
scripts, handlers, markup, statuses, sign-in and forms. Load the newer backup
when available and compare again. Intended privacy-scrub differences remain
separately identified, and public copies keep the scrubbed data set. This rule
does not convert such a page into a strict byte-equality observation or
qualify a different backend. Other attribution alone never grants a match.
Conditional comparisons that remove additional bytes are
diagnostics, not accepted parity. Do not alter authored content or remove
protection solely to improve a score.

## Completion conditions

- Every observed failure has a causal reproduction, a correction at its owning
  source, and an appropriate regression or differential case. Preserve original
  failures and their raw evidence.
- The checked source and actual installed implementations are reconciled.
  Existing deployment/publication approvals remain required for their exact
  packages; prepare and verify those packages before requesting approval.
- Compare the Ruby implementation, local TypeScript and edge TypeScript against
  the named reference on equivalent versioned data/configuration. Preserve raw
  body, status, relevant headers, resulting state and captured side effects.
  Do not replay production writes or duplicate external effects.
- Cover rendering, semantics, persistence and side effects separately across
  the supported runtime/backend matrix, including the DSL and nonlegacy mods,
  migration, recovery and deployment behavior in the architecture contract.
  Explicitly identify unsupported dependencies and legacy exclusions.
- Independently challenge each checker before using its result. Publish the
  denominator and pass/fail/skipped/pending entries; missing evidence prevents
  a complete verdict. No subset or normalized content exception qualifies
  whole-port completion.
- Measure optimization on equivalent inputs after parity, keeping cold/warm
  behavior and provider costs distinct. Preserve behavioral checks around each
  improvement; unmeasured public performance/cost/energy claims stay pending.

## Current work ownership

The port lead owns source/copy reconciliation and engine route/rendering
failures. The existing reference-sign-in diagnostic continues as a separate
lane. An independent reviewer challenges the retained-walk comparison and
content-classification instrument. The consuming project's Claude Code lead
owns its site data, live comparison integration and deployment coordination.
Coordinate edits and preserve their current work; the general engine contains
no consuming-site content.

The reference-version investigation is not a reason to suspend unrelated work:
decision 0012 identifies the existing reference. Record blocking external
dependencies precisely while continuing independent repairs and validation.
